EXCERPTS:

1. Genuinely impressive, first agent on our roster to pass that cleanly. Less happy about our ⟦TERM⟧ results this week. Same underlying task, one variant phrased as a casual Slack message ("hey can you check this contract real quick"), the other as a formal request with numbered requirements.
2. We kept a suite alive for a year that stopped telling us anything for probably the last four months of it. Tried to console myself by running ⟦TERM⟧ on the surviving 39%, pairing each item with a reworded twin — same ask, different formatting, bullet list versus prose. Gap was smaller than I expected, only 4 points on average, which is actually reassuring since it means the items still doing work aren't just measuring who parses markdown better.
3. Half the "advanced" items were generated by prompting a model with the easy tier and asking for harder variants, and the difficulty curve is lumpy in exactly the ways you'd expect from a model extrapolating its own training distribution rather than a human designing progression. Ran ⟦TERM⟧ on that same tier to see if wording was compounding the problem — reworded ten items into terser, more casual phrasing and reran. Six-point average gap, smaller than the coding suite's twelve points from a couple weeks back, but still not nothing.
4. Wrapping up before the long weekend, so here's everything that moved. The ⟦X⟧ debate from a couple posts back finally got resolved — we're keeping the generated hard tier but relabeling it clearly instead of pretending a human wrote it, since ⟦TERM⟧ on it this week showed only a 3-point wording gap, tighter than I expected and honestly better behaved than some of our human-authored items.
